{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Who Counts as Unemployed?","claim":"Whether someone counts as unemployed depends on defined classification criteria.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Explain who is counted as unemployed—and therefore included in the unemployment-rate denominator—through two contrasting situations: a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall and someone who only browsed job listings.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The title frames the criteria for classification, which the source defines and the narrative explicitly makes the story’s focus.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s central subject."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate is calculated using the number of unemployed people and the number of employed people—not everyone who lacks a job.","claim":"The unemployment rate uses unemployed people as its numerator and employed plus unemployed people, rather than all people without a job, as its denominator.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the combined number of employed and unemployed people, rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source specifies the numerator and denominator. The piece’s contrast with everyone lacking a job follows from that denominator.","materiality":"Defines the rate’s calculation and population."},{"excerpt":"Its denominator is the combined total of employed and unemployed people.","claim":"The rate’s denominator is the sum of employed and unemployed people.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"The rate’s denominator is the number of employed people plus unemployed people.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The denominator matches the source’s stated sum.","materiality":"Core definition of the rate’s denominator."},{"excerpt":"A person on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled may qualify as unemployed without actively searching for work.","claim":"A temporarily laid-off person expecting recall may be classified as unemployed without actively searching for work.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"An unemployed person was ... [someone who] made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source gives temporary layoff with expected recall as an alternative to active job search; “may” appropriately avoids implying automatic classification without the other criteria.","materiality":"Central exception to the active-search requirement."},{"excerpt":"By contrast, someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings does not meet the active-search requirement.","claim":"Browsing job listings as one’s only job-search activity does not satisfy the active-search requirement.","category":"technical","label":"U insufficient evidence to settle","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"Someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings. The first may qualify without an active search; passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source establishes that passive-only search does not count, but does not state whether browsing listings alone is passive-only; that specific example cannot be settled from the source.","materiality":"The example is central to the contrast and could affect how readers classify that activity."},{"excerpt":"In general, a person must not have been employed during the survey’s reference week","claim":"A person must not have been employed during the survey reference week to qualify as unemployed.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"An unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week","narrative":"The person must not have been employed during the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The employment-status condition and reference period match the source.","materiality":"A defining eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"and must have been available for work, except in the case of temporary illness.","claim":"A person must have been available for work, with temporary illness as an exception.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"was available for work except for temporary illness","narrative":"must have been available for work, except for temporary illness","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The condition and exception match the source.","materiality":"A defining eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"They must also have actively searched for work during the four-week period ending with that reference week","claim":"An active job search must have occurred during the four-week period ending with the survey reference week.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week","narrative":"Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The search window and its endpoint match the source.","materiality":"Specifies the time window for the active-search condition."},{"excerpt":"unless they were temporarily laid off and expected recall.","claim":"Temporary layoff with expected recall exempts a person from the active-search requirement.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall","narrative":"unless the person is temporarily laid off and expects recall","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source states this as an alternative to active search.","materiality":"Defines an exception to a central eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"These conditions determine whether someone is counted among the unemployed—and included in the rate’s denominator.","claim":"The stated conditions determine whether someone is classified as unemployed, and people classified as unemployed are included in the unemployment-rate denominator.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall. ... The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"The reader should understand that unemployment is not simply the absence of a job: classification depends on being available and, in most cases, actively searching during the specified period. The rate’s denominator is the number of employed people plus unemployed people.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source defines who is unemployed and separately specifies that unemployed people are part of the denominator; the sentence accurately links those facts.","materiality":"Summarizes how classification relates to inclusion in the rate."}]}

